# configs/ru-exclude.txt.example
#
# PURPOSE: Operator-defined CIDR ranges to exclude from the downloaded RU IP list.
# When /etc/ru-exclude.txt is present on the RPi, update-vpn-routes appends
# &exclude[cidr4]=CIDR query parameters to RU_SUBNET_URL before the curl download,
# so those ranges are filtered server-side and never appear in /etc/white-list.txt.
# Excluded CIDRs are therefore routed through the VPN (not the ISP).
#
# FORMAT: One CIDR per line. Lines starting with # are comments and are skipped.
# Blank lines are skipped. No inline comments after a CIDR — the whole line is
# used as-is in the URL query parameter.
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   1. Run: ssh pi4 "sudo /etc/vpn-status.sh --via=isp" to identify IPs exiting
#      via ISP that should go through the VPN (e.g. Google, Cloudflare ranges
#      that iplist incorrectly marks as RU).
#   2. Resolve the destination IP to a CIDR (e.g. via whois or ipinfo.io).
#   3. Copy this file to configs/ru-exclude.txt:
#        cp configs/ru-exclude.txt.example configs/ru-exclude.txt
#      (Note: configs/ru-exclude.txt is gitignored — it will NOT be committed.)
#   4. Add your CIDRs to configs/ru-exclude.txt (one per line, whole-line # comments only).
#   5. Run: ./deploy.sh — Stage 21 will SCP the file to /etc/ru-exclude.txt on the RPi.
#      The next update-vpn-routes run (cron or manual) will fetch the filtered list.
#
# NOTE: Changing the exclusion list changes the effective download URL, which changes
# the SHA256 of the downloaded file. The next update-vpn-routes run will detect the
# hash mismatch and rebuild routes automatically — no manual intervention needed.
#
# EXAMPLE ENTRIES (commented out — remove the leading # to activate):
# 142.250.0.0/16
# 142.251.0.0/16
